window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["0b"]={"https://theconversation.com/stable-government-is-parliaments-first-priority-a-fresh-election-isnt-the-answer-296669":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"2f54537f7e11fc67555be2bf6d0b0ea548fbc6d93f1d7349543758580fb2b53d","read":"https://theconversation.com/stable-government-is-parliaments-first-priority-a-fresh-election-isnt-the-answer-296669","bytes":179574,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Stable government is parliament’s first priority – a fresh election isn’t the answer","by":[{"n":"Grant Duncan","r":"Research Associate, Public Policy Institute, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"}],"pub":"2026-11-17T00:08:57Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["What if New Zealanders don’t get a new government for Christmas?"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s a common question after an inconclusive election that hinges on final results, due on November 27, and a possible recalculation of seat numbers."]},{"k":"p","s":["An election is competitive, but the government-formation process that follows calls for dialogue and compromise to achieve stable agreements that serve the country. Cross-party collaboration is exactly what the ",["proportional representation system","https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp"]," was intended to encourage."]},{"k":"p","s":["For the time being, New Zealand is under a ",["caretaker government","https://theconversation.com/nz-will-have-a-caretaker-government-for-weeks-what-that-means-and-how-it-works-295750"]," led by incumbent Prime Minister Christopher Luxon. It’s unclear who will lead the next government or when coalition agreements will be signed."]},{"k":"p","s":["There’s one hard deadline: parliament must assemble by January 14, whether or not a new government has been sworn in. Ideally, things would be resolved by that date."]},{"k":"p","s":["But suppose one or two parties refuse to compromise and no one has a majority. There’s been much talk – not all of it helpful – about a “ ",["hung parliament","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2567881/explainer-what-is-a-hung-parliament-and-could-it-actually-lead-to-a-new-election"],"” and even a fresh election."]},{"k":"p","s":["In fact, New Zealand’s constitutional conventions provide ample scope for political negotiations to arrive at a governance arrangement, supported by a majority in the House of Representatives."]},{"k":"p","s":["And there are strong incentives for the parties to preserve stable government. Any party blamed for a return to the polls is likely to be punished by voters, so it’s in all their interests to make it work."]},{"k":"h","s":["3 possible pathways"]},{"k":"p","s":["A temporary deadlock does not necessarily mean a fresh election. The question of who will govern can be settled in the debating chamber."]},{"k":"p","s":["After the 1928 election, for instance, the incumbent Reform government lost a no-confidence vote in the house and a minority United government (holding fewer than half the seats) took office – with the support of both parties’ natural adversary, the Labour Party."]},{"k":"p","s":["If an impasse occurs this summer, Luxon’s caretaker government remains in office. But because some ministers lost their seats in the election and their warrants expire by December 4, there would likely be a cabinet reshuffle."]},{"k":"p","s":["What happens if deadlocks in coalition negotiations mean no new government can be sworn in by the time the new parliament assembles?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Members of parliament would choose a speaker (who may be from any party) at the first meeting. The ",["Address in Reply debate","https://www3.parliament.nz/mi/get-involved/features/what-is-the-address-in-reply-debate/"]," is then the occasion for a confidence vote."]},{"k":"p","s":["That vote may reveal the incumbent coalition government has lost the confidence of the house and would have to resign. Then again, it may not. Here are the possible pathways."]},{"k":"li","s":["the three parties of Luxon’s caretaker government (assuming there’s been no split among them) hold a majority of seats and can defeat any motion of no confidence, in which case they continue to govern"]},{"k":"li","s":["the parties of the caretaker government don’t hold a majority but defeat the no-confidence motion anyway because a fourth party abstains or votes in their favour, in which case the coalition may continue to govern for the time being"]},{"k":"li","s":["the parties of the caretaker government don’t hold a majority and they lose the no-confidence motion, in which case the prime minister tenders his resignation to Governor-General Cindy Kiro."]},{"k":"h","s":["No need for the ‘nuclear option’"]},{"k":"p","s":["If the incumbent government resigns, the governor general would expect a workable coalition to be found as soon as possible, and to publish its agreements so a new administration can be sworn in."]},{"k":"p","s":["That means, in effect, a change of government without another election. A new coalition government could be led by Luxon or Labour’s Crhis Hipkins (or, for all we know, any other MP)."]},{"k":"p","s":["What if an alternative administration that commands the confidence of the house can’t be put together?"]},{"k":"p","s":["If the governor-general is left without “responsible advisers” (that is, government ministers), and no prime minister-designate appears to have the confidence of the house, she may have to use ",["reserve powers","https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/1-sovereign-governor-general-and-executive-council/governor-general"]," to dissolve the parliament and sign the writ for a fresh election."]},{"k":"p","s":["That “nuclear option” hasn’t been necessary before in New Zealand. It’s a generally accepted convention that the governor-general should not be put in a position that requires the use of reserve powers."]},{"k":"p","s":["If an early dissolution and election are ever necessary, they should be called for by the political parties, or by a majority of MPs. That way, the decision is transparently theirs and the governor-general’s ",["non-political constitutional role","https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/1-sovereign-governor-general-and-executive-council/governor-general"]," is protected."]},{"k":"p","s":["The politicians are then accountable to the people for having failed to form a stable government – and will be judged at the ballot boxes. That may only make it harder to form a new government, however."]},{"k":"p","s":["A majority can always be found, when the will is there. The governor-general must always have responsible advisers and the people need stable government – even if that government isn’t what most people would have wanted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Stable government can take time"]},{"k":"p","s":["Forming a new government can take a long time and prolonged periods of caretaker government have happened elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["Belgium holds the world record. Culturally and politically divided between Flemish-speaking Flanders and French-speaking Wallonia, it has seen radical left- and right-wing parties grow, causing problems in forming majority governments."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2010-11, the country went 589 days ",["without forming a new federal government","https://www.progressivepolicy.org/how-belgium-survived-20-months-without-a-government/"],". In September 2020, a seven-party coalition agreement ",["ended a record 652 days of caretaker government","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/sep/30/belgium-agrees-on-government-nearly-two-years-after-previous-one-fell"],". Despite this, life went on and public administration continued."]},{"k":"p","s":["But it’s not best practice to have a caretaker or minority government battling from one confidence vote to the next and struggling to get the votes to pass a budget."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s far preferable that enough parties sign coalition agreements to ensure majority support for the full parliamentary term. If they can’t, public confidence declines, the parties argue more, and it only gets harder next time."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the political actors behave responsibly and make some compromises, they could have this job wrapped up by Christmas."]}],"chars":5974},"https://theconversation.com/eurovision-asia-a-diverse-and-spectacular-showcase-of-asian-pop-294346":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"276d0a0a08a2b1dda26a6debbf0c8431311041e4c8960b5cbb2d93789c617c35","read":"https://theconversation.com/eurovision-asia-a-diverse-and-spectacular-showcase-of-asian-pop-294346","bytes":189044,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Eurovision Asia: a diverse and spectacular showcase of Asian pop","by":[{"n":"Jess Carniel","r":"Associate Professor in Humanities, University of Southern Queensland"}],"pub":"2026-11-16T04:44:27Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["On November 14, Asia fired up its wind machines and pyrotechnics for the inaugural Eurovision Song Contest Asia, aka Eurovision Asia."]},{"k":"p","s":["Eleven countries competed – from Bangladesh and Bhutan to Vietnam and host country Thailand. Notably absent this first year were most of the continent’s most populous nations: China, India, Japan, Pakistan and Indonesia."]},{"k":"p","s":["The format was familiar: three-minute songs performed with no more than six people permitted on stage. But there was a distinct regional inflection in the staging, costuming and language choices."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional flavour"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many of the artists wove in elements of ",["traditional sound","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V96Bd3Bqt60"]," and ",["traditional dress","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sjZD9c4mxXk&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=8&pp=iAQB8AUB"],", and artists expressed gratitude to the audience with the traditional Thai gesture wai (hands together)."]},{"k":"p","s":["While the original Eurovision is hosted in a combination of English and French, Eurovision Asia was delivered in English only. But most artists performed either in their own language (36%) or a combination of that language with English (54%). Only South Korea’s song was performed entirely in English."]},{"k":"p","s":["By comparison, Eurovision 2026 featured 43% of songs entirely in English, 38% in languages other than English, and 19% multilingual."]},{"k":"p","s":["Musically, it was a diverse offering. The delicate lotus leaves of ",["Vietnam’s Tóc Tiên","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4flXEWQe_rs&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=3&pp=iAQB8AUB"]," and her back-up dancers broke into a high-energy opening number, Em Tiên. Malaysia’s Aina Abdul finished the show with a ",["pitch-perfect power ballad","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wk6BMLVwGfY&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=4&pp=iAQB8AUB"],", Bluest Sky."]},{"k":"p","s":["In between, audiences were treated to Cambodian and ",["Bhutanese ballads","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kis5nKAdhsM&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=6&pp=iAQB8AUB0gcJCUoMAYcqIYzv"],", ",["Bengali a capella","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AKSQsQF6fek&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=11&pp=iAQB8AUB"],", South Korean K-pop and, perhaps most surprisingly, ",["Nepali rock","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-v8vDtmEo4M&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=9&pp=iAQB8AUB"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Girls to the front"]},{"k":"p","s":["But it was the Philippines’ Carmelle – the youngest contestant, at 19 – who won the night with ",["Sobra-Sobra","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ccfIW1zsNdM"],". The focus of the empowering pop song in English and Tagalog was about Filipino women’s right to be who they want to be."]},{"k":"p","s":["“It’s about how unapologetic, independent and strong Filipinas are. It describes me – not just me, but of course all the Filipinas,” ",["Carmelle told The Guardian","https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/nov/14/philippines-carmelle-wins-first-eurovision-contest-in-asia"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Carmelle received 211 points, winning both the jury and the public vote, encompassing viewers from 183 countries and territories."]},{"k":"p","s":["With her victory, the Philippines has won the right to host Eurovision Asia in 2027. In the meantime, Carmelle will also perform at the 2027 Eurovision Song Contest in Bulgaria."]},{"k":"h","s":["Spectacle and snags"]},{"k":"p","s":["Eurovision is known for its spectacle, and Eurovision Asia delivered, albeit on a more ",["modest budget","https://escxtra.com/2026/10/28/eurovision-song-contest-asia-2026-in-production-crisis/"],". Stage production made the most of stunning visual projections, smoke and flares."]},{"k":"p","s":["Eurovision is also known for its non-political stance, with political or ideological messages prohibited."]},{"k":"p","s":["But it has been ",["more forgiving toward humanitarian statements","https://theconversation.com/eurovision-under-the-shadow-of-war-how-the-2023-contest-highlighted-humanitarianism-empathy-and-solidarity-205468"],". And at Eurovision Asia, Nepal’s Pratik Himal Rai used the end of his set to call for support for his country as it recovers from the ",["August flood disaster","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/10/1168651"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The show was not without its hitches. Laos’ representative, ",["Thinla","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4JpQcPTwYKA&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=13"],", couldn’t hear her music due to an issue with her earpiece, so footage of her technical rehearsal was shown at the end of the performances to ensure fair judgement of her act."]},{"k":"p","s":["Thai artists ",["Mang x Daou","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V96Bd3Bqt60&list=PLcrsSbhq1-74&index=9"]," experienced wardrobe malfunctions during their ballad Aroma, though this didn’t affect their chances in the voting – Thailand came in second place, with 169 points."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why not Asiavision?"]},{"k":"p","s":["If Europe has Eurovision, why can’t Asia have Asiavision?"]},{"k":"p","s":["For one, the name “Asiavision” is already taken: it’s the broadcasting network of the ",["Asia-Pacific Broadcasting Union","https://www.abu.org.my/"]," (ABU), which has its own song contests and ",["festivals","https://www.abu.org.my/abu-song-festival-2025/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["But mostly, it’s about branding. Although the name Eurovision has become synonymous with the song contest, it actually refers to the European Broadcasting Union’s (EBU) pan-European telecommunications network."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Eurovision Song Contest was created in 1956 to promote the network and attract audiences across borders. (No, it was ",["not created","https://www.scienceandmediamuseum.org.uk/objects-and-stories/brief-history-eurovision-broadcast-technology"]," to unite a war-torn Europe.)"]},{"k":"p","s":["The purpose of Eurovision Asia is specifically to expand the Eurovision brand and format into the region. This is a project the EBU has been ",["attempting for more than two decades","https://eurovoix.com/2026/03/31/the-eurovision-song-contest-asia/"],". It plans to continue the expansion of the brand through other ",["continental editions","https://eurovoix.com/2026/04/04/ebu-to-focus-on-continental-versions-of-eurovision-for-future-expansion/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["The future of Eurovision Asia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Staging the inaugural competition is one thing, but securing the longevity of the franchise in Asia is another matter."]},{"k":"p","s":["Buy-in from ",["economic powerhouses Japan, China and India","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/world/asia/eurovision-asia-china-japan.html"]," may be the key to its success. Negotiations were underway with China and Japan to participate this year, and organisers are ",["optimistic","https://eurovoix.com/2026/11/08/seventeen-countries-interested-in-competing-in-eurovision-asia-2027/"]," that they and several others will join in 2027."]},{"k":"p","s":["The broad geographical scope of “Asia” also has the potential to draw in participants from the Middle East."]},{"k":"p","s":["But broader participation presents its own challenges."]},{"k":"p","s":["The original Eurovision is heavily associated with LGBTQIA+ acceptance and pride, which may not be well-received by certain broadcasters and their audiences within Asia."]},{"k":"p","s":["We saw this play out in the inaugural Eurovision Asia contest: Malaysian broadcaster TV3 ",["cut drag performer Vice Ganda","https://eurovoix.com/2026/11/15/%F0%9F%87%B2%F0%9F%87%BE-malaysia-tv3-alters-eurovision-asia-broadcast-to-remove-drag-queen/"],", the Phillipines’ jury spokesperson, from its broadcast."]},{"k":"p","s":["There was also speculation about whether Islamophobia played a role in Malaysia’s ",["Aina Abdul’s results","https://manilastandard.net/showbiz/digital-media/314818693/eurovision-asia-voting-sparks-islamophobia-debate-after-malaysias-loss.html#:%7E:text=The%20online%20debate%20also%20raised,a%20role%20in%20the%20outcome."],"; despite being a favourite to win going into the contest, she finished in fourth place."]},{"k":"h","s":["Soft power and pop"]},{"k":"p","s":["Like the OG Eurovision, Eurovision Asia offers great potential for ",["soft power politics","https://theconversation.com/how-asiavision-could-be-a-boon-for-cultural-diplomacy-56988"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["It also has the potential to be a powerful global platform for Asian pop. The inaugural competition was a diverse showcase for the unique voices and cultures of its participants, beyond the dominant trends of J- and K-pop. Hopefully we will get a chance to see more in Manila next year."]}],"chars":5596},"https://theconversation.com/hegseth-has-survived-an-attempt-to-oust-him-for-now-a-much-bigger-fight-looms-296295":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"8c384b96558256608dd3dd9eec50974125ec1b9334d9b365861ebd55d3eef5cb","read":"https://theconversation.com/hegseth-has-survived-an-attempt-to-oust-him-for-now-a-much-bigger-fight-looms-296295","bytes":189418,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Hegseth has survived an attempt to oust him – for now. A much bigger fight looms","by":[{"n":"Ava Kalinauskas","r":"Senior Research Associate, United States Studies Centre, University of Sydney"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T03:33:59Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["As a historic “ ",["blue wave","https://theconversation.com/the-democrats-blue-tsunami-is-a-strong-repudiation-of-donald-trump-now-they-will-look-to-replicate-it-in-2028-295856"],"” swept the Democrats to victory in last week’s US midterms, the likely incoming House oversight committee chair, Democratic lawmaker Robert Garcia, had a ",["simple message","https://oversightdemocrats.house.gov/news/press-releases/incoming-oversight-chairman-robert-garcia-statement-after-democrats-secure-majority-in-the-us-house-of-representatives"]," for US President Donald Trump and his allies: “Lawyer up”."]},{"k":"p","s":["But before the new Democrat-controlled Congress has even been sworn in, it’s a member of Trump’s own party who has caused the latest headache for his administration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On Monday, outgoing Kentucky Representative Thomas Massie, a Republican, took to the House floor to ",["formally introduce","https://www.smh.com.au/world/north-america/republican-congressman-files-resolution-to-impeach-pete-hegseth-20261110-p61crg.html"]," a 34-page impeachment resolution against Pete Hegseth, the US secretary of defense."]},{"k":"p","s":["Albeit an extraordinary rebuke, the move was unlikely to succeed, with Republicans still in power until January."]},{"k":"p","s":["And indeed, on Tuesday, the Republican-led House voted ",["211-204","https://www.politico.com/news/2026/11/10/house-impeachment-hegseth-01160216"]," to refer it to committee, effectively killing off the measure for the remainder of this Congress. Hegseth has survived this attempt to oust him."]},{"k":"p","s":["Any reprieve, however, may be short-lived. With the Democrats preparing to take control of Congress, how does impeachment of a cabinet official work? And could Hegseth still become the first US defence secretary in history to be removed from office?"]},{"k":"h","s":["What is impeachment?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Nearly 240 years ago, the framers of the US Constitution, fearful of tyranny, wanted a legislative process that could check abuses of executive power and remove a president or official who committed them."]},{"k":"p","s":["Article II, Section 4 of the Constitution ",["reads","https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artII-S4-1/ALDE_00000282/"],":"]},{"k":"p","s":["The President, Vice President and all civil Officers of the United States, shall be removed from Office on Impeachment for, and Conviction of, Treason, Bribery, or other high Crimes and Misdemeanors."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Constitution","https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/constitution-transcript"]," vests the House with “the sole Power of Impeachment”, which takes only a simple majority."]},{"k":"p","s":["But impeachment only represents the formal process of laying charges. Removal from office is a much taller order, and requires a conviction through a trial in the Senate, with a ",["two-thirds supermajority","https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/constitution-transcript"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In other words, 67 of the 100 senators must vote to convict for the individual to be removed."]},{"k":"h","s":["Not an ordinary courtroom"]},{"k":"p","s":["Unlike standard criminal or civil court proceedings with strict burdens of proof, the grounds for impeachment remain vague."]},{"k":"p","s":["US Founding Father Alexander Hamilton ",["characterised","https://teachingamericanhistory.org/document/the-federalist/"]," impeachable offences as those stemming from “the misconduct of public men, or in other words, from the abuse or violation of some public trust”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ultimately, as one academic ",["put it","https://www.ussc.edu.au/impeachment-101-the-history-process-and-prospect-of-a-trump-impeachment"],":"]},{"k":"p","s":["Impeachment is a political process, albeit with legal overtones."]},{"k":"p","s":["And while it’s been back in the ",["spotlight","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/majority-of-house-members-vote-for-2nd-impeachment-of-trump"]," in the Trump era, impeachment is still exceptionally rare — especially for cabinet officials. Only two US cabinet secretaries have ever been impeached, and neither was ultimately convicted and removed from office."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2024, during the Biden administration, US Secretary of Homeland Security Alejandro Mayorkas was ",["impeached by House Republicans","https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-68286641"]," over enforcement of immigration policy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then-President Joe Biden ",["denounced the vote","https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/biden-denounce-mayorkas-impeachment-effort-baseless-unconstitutional-rcna137301"],", which fell on party lines, as “baseless”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The only other case occurred back in the ",["Gilded Age","https://theconversation.com/friday-essay-trumps-presidency-is-being-compared-to-americas-gilded-age-what-was-it-and-what-happened-next-251401"],". In 1876, the House voted unanimously to impeach US Secretary of War William Belknap over ",["corruption allegations","https://history.house.gov/Historical-Highlights/1851-1900/The-Impeachment-of-Secretary-William-Belknap/"],", despite the fact that Belknap had ",["resigned","https://www.yalejreg.com/nc/late-impeachment-an-in-depth-account-of-the-belknap-by-thomas-berry-part-one/"]," from office just a few hours before."]},{"k":"h","s":["What were the allegations against Hegseth?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Massie’s resolution contained ",["eight articles of impeachment","https://massie.house.gov/uploadedfiles/hegseth_articles_of_impeachment.pdf"],", which claimed Hegseth has abused his powers through unlawful acts – “especially with regard to the commission of war”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most articles accuse Hegseth of waging war without congressional approval. Among the most notable examples, it cites the operation to capture Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro back in January, and the launch of military action against Iran less than two months later."]},{"k":"p","s":["Massie is a ",["long-term","https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/11/09/5-things-know-about-rep-massie-who-introduced-articles-impeach-hegseth/"]," and ",["outspoken","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cwy2vw8j0ddo"]," libertarian critic of Trump."]},{"k":"p","s":["After being toppled by a Trump-endorsed primary challenger earlier this year, he will no longer represent Kentucky’s fourth congressional district in the incoming Congress."]},{"k":"p","s":["With little to lose, and in the final months of a Republican-controlled Congress, Massie ",["said","https://www.npr.org/2026/11/09/nx-s1-6034483/thomas-massie-impeachment-pete-hegseth"]," he was “doing this because it’s the right thing to do”."]},{"k":"h","s":["The political price of war"]},{"k":"p","s":["But the timing also reflects the political fallout from the Iran war, which many Republicans have ",["blamed","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/crz98zqkz7z0o"]," for their devastating rout at the midterms."]},{"k":"p","s":["In recent months, ",["survey","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/10/08/iran-conflict-remains-deeply-unpopular-as-many-americans-expect-it-to-go-on-for-months/"]," after survey has found the vast majority of Americans disapprove of the government’s handling of the conflict and are concerned about ",["higher gas and fuel prices","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2026/10/08/iran-conflict-remains-deeply-unpopular-as-many-americans-expect-it-to-go-on-for-months/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["A YouGov ",["poll","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/10/poll-finds-voters-preferred-candidates-who-opposed-iran-war-by-3-1-margin"]," conducted on the eve of the midterm elections found a candidate’s support for the Iran war was more than three times as likely to put voters off as win them over."]},{"k":"p","s":["It wouldn’t be the first time an unpopular war has cost a Pentagon official their job. In 2006, President George W Bush announced Defense Secretary Donald Rumsfeld’s resignation the day after Democrats won back Congress in a bruising midterm defeat for the Republicans."]},{"k":"p","s":["This had been largely driven by ",["public discontent","https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2006/10/05/iraq-looms-large-in-nationalized-election/"]," with the Iraq war."]},{"k":"p","s":["Against this backdrop, Massie was likely betting that the midterm result has given Republican lawmakers – including ",["some 70 House representatives","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-10/us-defence-secretary-pete-hegseth-subject-of-new-impeachment-bid/107366698"]," who will be vacating their seats in January – a renewed willingness to flex their legislative muscles in the twilight of this Congress."]},{"k":"h","s":["Controlling the fall"]},{"k":"p","s":["Massie has argued that Hegseth’s removal from office could provide Republicans the opportunity to confirm a replacement before the Democratic Party takes over both chambers. This, he has ",["said","https://www.npr.org/2026/11/09/nx-s1-6034483/thomas-massie-impeachment-pete-hegseth"],", would be:"]},{"k":"p","s":["a much gentler version of what’s coming when the Democrats assume control of the House and Senate next year."]},{"k":"p","s":["Chances are, he’s right. Expected incoming Speaker of the House Hakeem Jeffries has ",["previously signalled","https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/2026/10/democrats-impeachment-plans-hegseth-pentagon/689195/"]," that targeting the high-profile cabinet official would be top of the party’s to-do list in a Democratic-controlled Congress:"]},{"k":"p","s":["One of the first things we’re going to do in the majority, we’re going to run Pete Hegseth out of town."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ahead of Tuesday’s vote, Jeffries ",["declared himself","https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/11/08/pete-hegseth-impeachment-would-be-hard-yes-hakeem-jeffries/"]," a “hard yes” on Massie’s resolution and anticipated it would enjoy “incredibly strong support” from his party colleagues."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why conviction remains unlikely"]},{"k":"p","s":["Democrats could be primed to mount their own similar case against Hegseth when they take control of the chamber in January."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, even if next year an impeachment resolution can sail through the new Democratic-majority House, it is still unlikely to clear the high two-thirds bar in the Senate required for conviction. For his part, Trump has ",["vowed","https://edition.cnn.com/2026/11/09/politics/live-news/midterm-election-trump-congress?post-id=cmw9u0yey00003b6unlkk6mzv"]," to “stand by” Hegseth."]},{"k":"p","s":["But Hegseth may still end up leaving office, one way or another. As one ",["researcher","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/the-midterm-effect-what-the-2026-election-could-mean-for-public-policy/"]," predicted before the midterms, any impeachments of cabinet officers that proceed to trial with even modest Republican support could force those officials to resign."]},{"k":"p","s":["Whatever happens, this ordeal is a first taste of what’s to come for the Trump administration over the next two years. And the Democrats barely had to lift a finger."]}],"chars":6938},"https://theconversation.com/the-history-of-royal-tours-shows-why-theyre-important-for-the-monarchy-despite-the-risk-of-uncomfortable-questions-295479":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"c3ac76d14867c918d2be3412560d928449384e6e17d60463e77879a618f5115e","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-history-of-royal-tours-shows-why-theyre-important-for-the-monarchy-despite-the-risk-of-uncomfortable-questions-295479","bytes":183095,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The history of royal tours shows why they’re important for the monarchy – despite the risk of uncomfortable questions","by":[{"n":"Alice Hunt","r":"Professor of Early Modern Literature and History, University of Southampton"}],"pub":"2026-11-02T14:52:03Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The royal tour of the Caribbean is the king’s first visit to this region of the Commonwealth as monarch. It comes at a time of protests and growing demands for slavery reparations, as well as an apology for the royal family’s historic role in the trade."]},{"k":"p","s":["Against this backdrop, ",["historian","https://theconversation.com/topics/history-180"]," Anthony Seldon ",["described this tour","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckzd6gvg1r2jo"]," as potentially “the biggest risk” of the king’s reign. Charles is in the region not just as a head of state and of the Commonwealth, but as the head of a family that personally benefited from slavery."]},{"k":"p","s":["Prime Minister Andy Burnham has also travelled to the Caribbean for the Commonwealth heads of government summit. Burnham has said he is open to the idea of ",["an apology","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5398239je88o"]," for Britain’s role in the slave trade, but has made clear that he does not believe reparations are the right answer."]},{"k":"p","s":["The last time Charles visited the Caribbean was in November 2021, to oversee the transition of Barbados to a republic. The Bahamas and Antigua and Barbuda, on the schedule this time, are among the eight Caribbean nations which retain the British monarch as head of state. Guyana, also part of the tour, became a republic in 1970."]},{"k":"p","s":["The king knew he could not escape uncomfortable exchanges on this tour. There is increasing local pressure from the ",["Caribbean Reparations Commission","https://caricomreparations.org/"]," on Charles to offer a formal apology for Britain’s role in the slave trade. On the king’s first night in the Bahamas, at a youth rally in the capital Nassau, ",["he referred","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmwyv8ld22z1o"]," to “the great suffering and injustices of the past”. There were some protesters, but they remained outside."]},{"k":"p","s":["Royal tours have always been risky. After the last royal visit to the Caribbean, to mark the platinum jubilee in 2022, the traditional format looked to be in need of a rethink. ",["“Tone deaf”","https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/william-kate-jamaica-royals-b2042682.html"]," images of William and Kate in Jamaica, dressed in imperial white in an open-top Jamaica Defence Force Land Rover, and greeting children through a wire fence went viral."]},{"k":"p","s":["It was a jarring reminder of the colonial past, overshadowing the entire tour, which also saw protests in Belize and the Bahamas. Afterwards, William said that foreign tours offered ",["“an opportunity to reflect”","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-60890440"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["The historic role of the tour"]},{"k":"p","s":["The risks of this autumn’s tour will have been weighed carefully by the Royal Visits Committee (part of the Cabinet Office). The history of the royal tour can explain why the risks were considered to be worth taking."]},{"k":"p","s":["As part of ",["“The Visible Crown”","https://www.visiblecrown.com/#Home-about"]," research project, I have examined the royal family’s connections to the Caribbean, looking in particular at visits since Elizabeth II took the throne in 1952. What I’ve found is that the survival of the monarchy in the Caribbean can in part be explained by the history of its carefully managed visibility."]},{"k":"p","s":["The royal visit as we know it was invented to legitimise the Commonwealth and sustain the British monarchy during the period of imperial decline and constitutional change."]},{"k":"p","s":["For some of the islands, Elizabeth’s tour to the Caribbean in 1966 was the first time a monarch had ever visited. At that time, officials threshed out their “philosophy of the royal visit”. They knew that the tours needed to present an image of Elizabeth that would make sense of her role in countries that had either become newly independent, or were about to become independent, as well as in those that might choose to become republics."]},{"k":"p","s":["That image would be as the mother figure for the new “family of nations” – the Commonwealth. The royal tour has remained largely unchanged since: visits should be short, but frequent – a change from the longer imperial tours. They should have the feel of a family party and the monarch must be easily visible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Informality and accessibility were priorities: garden parties, walkabouts in the sun and street celebrations as well as the more sombre guards of honour and speeches in the host nation’s parliament would become staples."]},{"k":"p","s":["The formulaic itineraries endorsed the values of continuity, protection and compassion. Children were always involved – visits to children’s homes, schools and hospitals afford opportunities to show a caring, healing monarch invested in their future."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Official footage","https://player.bfi.org.uk/free/film/watch-the-royal-tour-of-the-caribbean-1966-online"]," of the 1966 Caribbean tour pauses on a child in St Lucia holding a placard: “We love you, come back soon”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The choreographers of royal tours are aware of the strange capacity for these rituals to rouse emotion and cultivate loyalty. After Elizabeth’s visit to Jamaica in 1983, the country’s high commissioner ",["reported back","https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C15116"]," to the British government that “the warmth of popular feeling” generated by her visit demonstrated “afresh the widespread attachment to the crown”. Charles now has to remake the royal tour in his own image. But in fact, his age is on his side, lending him vulnerability."]},{"k":"p","s":["As my work on the ",["history of royal ceremonies","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/drama-of-coronation/B63DC86C42DC9CB9CD508A0F155BB1CC"]," has shown, the British monarchy has always deployed and adapted symbols, rituals and pageantry to legitimise itself and ",["sustain significance","https://www.faber.co.uk/product/9780571303212-republic/"]," in a changing world. The modern Commonwealth tour and its insistence on visibility is informed by the early modern tradition of the royal progress. Elizabeth I, like Elizabeth II, knew that she had to be ",["“seen to be believed”","https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/mar/29/we-have-to-be-seen-to-be-believed-the-endurance-of-the-royal-tour"],", and frequently moved through London and around the country greeting subjects. Then, as now, there were protests as well as cheers."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the context of the Caribbean however, a tour’s display of royal power reinforces a ",["complex and longstanding alignment","https://www.um.edu.mt/library/oar/handle/123456789/115059"]," between supposedly legitimate authority and whiteness. But constitutional reform was also part of the agenda of this current trip. Several Caribbean leaders have recently stated their intention for their nations to become republics: ",["Jamaica","https://roadtorepublic.mlca.gov.jm/author/j7vwyoaxwt/"],", ",["Bahamas","https://bahamaschronicle.com/the-bahamas-becomes-latest-commonwealth-country-to-signal-its-intention-to-hold-republic-referendum-in-wake-of-queens-death-months-after-william-signalled-monarchys-support-for-natio/"],", and ",["Antigua and Barbuda","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/sep/11/antigua-and-barbuda-republic-referendum-within-three-years-pm-queen-death"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["But as a ",["recent survey","https://www.visiblecrown.com/#Home-about"]," by researchers at the University of West Indies has revealed, support for republicanism varies significantly between nations. It is not strong enough, according to many of the constitutions, to bring about reform."]},{"k":"p","s":["The king’s (unelected) role as head of the Commonwealth matters to him, as it did to his mother. This position preserves the global status of the British monarchy. This tour was an opportunity, then, to bind the Caribbean Commonwealth, and to legitimise Charles as its father figure. He may not need to say sorry for Britain’s role in slavery for this trip to be considered a success."]},{"k":"p","s":["This article was updated on November 3 to amend some details about the constitutional arrangements of the countries visited on this tour."]}],"chars":6401},"https://theconversation.com/un-peacekeepers-prepare-to-exit-lebanon-after-70-years-leaving-behind-a-worrying-security-vacuum-293159":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"f9c7e76fcf5ad1a82443814c532bc4dffee007b5f42ae8f2f0d693dcb63eeddd","read":"https://theconversation.com/un-peacekeepers-prepare-to-exit-lebanon-after-70-years-leaving-behind-a-worrying-security-vacuum-293159","bytes":190215,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"UN peacekeepers prepare to exit Lebanon after 70 years – leaving behind a worrying security vacuum","by":[{"n":"Mireille Rebeiz","r":"Director of Dickinson Program in New Zealand, Dickinson College; University of Otago"}],"pub":"2026-10-23T18:45:07Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["For nearly 70 years, the blue helmets of United Nations’ peacekeepers have dotted the landscape of southern Lebanon. But those days may soon be over."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under Israeli and American pressure, the U.N. Security Council unanimously voted in August to end the mandate establishing ",["the United Nations Interim Force in Lebanon, or UNIFIL","https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/whatsinblue/2025/08/un-interim-force-in-lebanon-unifil-vote-on-final-mandate-renewal-and-drawdown.php"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["It means the 7,565 members of UNIFIL deployed in Lebanon – ",["representing about 46 countries","https://unifil.unmissions.org/en/unifil-troop-contributing-countries"]," – will depart the country no later than Dec. 31, 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["The end of the U.N. presence comes at a pivotal time. Israel and Lebanon have already held ",["seven rounds of negotiations","https://zoryaninstitute.org/scholar-corner/lebanon-israel-are-the-negotiations-dead-or-just-stalled/"]," aimed at bringing a lasting peace to a region that has suffered from decades of conflict."]},{"k":"p","s":["Brokered by the United States, these talks that led to the signing of the ",["Trilateral Framework in June 2026","https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesman/2026/06/trilateral-framework-between-the-united-states-of-america-the-state-of-israel-and-the-republic-of-lebanon"]," call for the disarmament of Hezbollah, the Israeli withdrawal from all Lebanese lands and the settlement of all disputes over borders. Yet all of these conditions are still some way off from completion – and ",["beset by a series of barriers","https://theconversation.com/hezbollah-degraded-weakened-but-not-yet-disarmed-destabilizes-lebanon-once-again-277327"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["As such, the departure of the United Nations peacekeeping forces raises serious concerns. The potential for a security vacuum looms large in south Lebanon, especially given that ",["Israel continues to bomb Lebanon","https://qna.org.qa/en/news/news-details?id=israeli-forces-launch-fresh-attacks-in-southern-lebanon&date=6/10/2026"]," despite a ",["ceasefire that has nominally been in place since April 16, 2026","https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/04/ten-day-cessation-of-hostilities-to-enable-peace-negotiations-between-israel-and-lebanon"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["As a ",["scholar of Middle East Studies","https://theconversation.com/profiles/mireille-rebeiz-1503505"],", I know that UNIFIL has never been a permanent solution. But I fear that its termination creates more problems than it solves, especially when there are no clear replacements or post-UNIFIL security arrangements in place."]},{"k":"h","s":["The UN in Lebanon"]},{"k":"p","s":["While the U.N. had had an ",["on-the-ground presence in Lebanon to handle civil strife since the 1950s","https://peacekeeping.un.org/sites/default/files/past/unogilbackgr.html"],", its role in mediating between Lebanon and Israel started later."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 1978, after a series of attacks on Israel organized by Palestinian fighters in Lebanon, Israel invaded and occupied the south in what was known as ",["Operation Litani","https://www.idf.il/en/mini-sites/wars-and-operations/operation-litani/"],". As a result, the United Nations Security Council ",["established UNIFIL","https://unifil.unmissions.org/en/unifil-timeline"]," to monitor Israel’s withdrawal from south Lebanon and to restore peace at the Lebanese–Israeli border."]},{"k":"p","s":["Israel’s occupation of south Lebanon lasted ",["more than two decades","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2000/may/24/israelandthepalestinians.lebanon"],". Following its withdrawal on May 25, 2000, the U.N. set the ",["Blue Line","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/02/1166875"]," – a 75-mile (120 km) demarcation line created for the practical purpose of confirming Israeli withdrawal from south Lebanon. It was not an official border but a buffer zone monitored by the U.N. peacekeepers."]},{"k":"p","s":["UNFIL and the Blue Line served as geographic and diplomatic focal points in the ",["2006 war","https://www.britannica.com/event/2006-Lebanon-War"]," between ",["Hezbollah","https://theconversation.com/does-hezbollah-represent-lebanon-and-what-impact-will-the-death-of-longtime-leader-hassan-nasrallah-have-240062"]," – an Iranian-backed militia operating from Lebanon – and Israel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Resolution 1701 of the Security Council, which brought an end to the 2006 war, ",["dramatically expanded the UNIFIL role in Lebanon","https://unifil.unmissions.org/en/unifil-timeline"],"; the number of troops went from 2,000 to 15,000 authorized military personnel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Its mandate also changed to include monitoring the ceasefire and documenting its violations, coordinating with Lebanon’s military and functioning as the only direct communication channel between the Lebanese and Israeli militaries. UNIFIL also offered humanitarian aid to the affected civilian population of south Lebanon."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet UNIFIL’s role never altered the basic lack of peace on the Israeli-Lebanese border. Over 50 years, ",["Israel invaded Lebanon numerous times","https://theconversation.com/israeli-threats-to-occupy-or-annex-south-lebanon-dust-off-a-decades-old-playbook-279704"],". And since 2000, ",["Hezbollah has flourished in south Lebanon","https://theconversation.com/does-hezbollah-represent-lebanon-and-what-impact-will-the-death-of-longtime-leader-hassan-nasrallah-have-240062"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["A checkered history of interventions"]},{"k":"p","s":["As UNIFIL prepares to depart, it’s unclear what will fill the looming security vacuum. It could be filled by non-U.N. troops, but Lebanon’s past shows the risks inherent to international peacekeeping missions."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 1982, after Israel invaded Lebanon and occupied Beirut with the intention of eliminating the Palestinian militant threat, Lebanese authorities called on Western powers for help. In August, the governments of the U.S., France, Italy and the United Kingdom created a ",["multinational peacekeeping force","https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/middle-east/lebanon.html"]," designed to restore peace and stop the fighting between the Lebanese, Palestinians and Israelis."]},{"k":"p","s":["But on ",["Oct. 23, 1983","https://www.marines.mil/News/Marines-TV/videoid/634642/dvpTag/Beirut/"],", the multinational peacekeeping force was the target of a dual suicide attack that killed 241 American service members and 58 French paratroopers."]},{"k":"p","s":["To this day, the attack – claimed by the pro-Iranian ",["Islamic Jihad","https://www.britannica.com/event/1983-Beirut-barracks-bombings"]," – remains the ",["deadliest single-day incident for the U.S. Marine Corps","https://www.arlingtoncemetery.mil/Explore/Monuments-and-Memorials/Beirut-Barracks"]," since the Battle of Iwo Jima in 1945."]},{"k":"p","s":["In addition to Israel, interventions in Lebanon have also come from another neighboring country."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 1976, the Syrian military intervened during Lebanon’s civil war to put an end to sectarian violence. Nearly three decades of occupation followed, and troops were withdrawn only following the ",["assassination of Lebanese Prime Minister Rafik Hariri in 2005","https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-60691507"]," and the subsequent ",["Cedar Revolution","https://nvdatabase.swarthmore.edu/content/lebanese-campaign-democracy-independence-intifada-or-cedar-revolution-2005"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["What next for Lebanon"]},{"k":"p","s":["With the absence of an internal Lebanese consensus, the arrival of any international peacekeeping force to replace the exiting UNIFIL will be perceived by many Lebanese as a top-down imposition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Moreover, the situation on the ground makes it dangerous for any international force to successfully operate. Israel is reportedly occupying ",["over 10% of Lebanon’s territories","https://apnews.com/projects/israel-expansion-maps/"],", ",["including","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cge41d19gq9o"]," multiple permanent bases and outposts in southern Lebanese territory."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hezbollah, meanwhile, ",["refuses to disarm","https://www.reutersconnect.com/item/lebanon-hezbollah-leader-reaffirms-refusal-to-disarm-vows-continued-resistance-against-israel/dGFnOnJldXRlcnMuY29tLDIwMjY6bmV3c21sX09XU0hCQzExOTAwNDM"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Finally, the fate of Lebanon-Israel ",["direct negotiations is unclear","https://zoryaninstitute.org/scholar-corner/lebanon-israel-are-the-negotiations-dead-or-just-stalled/"],". Recently, the countries held military talks in Florida, but ",["little is expected from them","https://www.naharnet.com/stories/323237-little-expected-from-lebanon-israel-military-talks-in-florida/print"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet a number of efforts are ongoing despite the difficult circumstances surrounding UNIFIL’s withdrawal. On June 25, French President Emmanuel Macron and Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni announced an “ ",["international coalition to support Lebanon","https://www.elysee.fr/en/emmanuel-macron/2026/06/25/franco-italian-joint-statement#"],"” to reinforce Lebanon’s sovereignty and help prevent a security vacuum in south Lebanon."]},{"k":"p","s":["In parallel, the ",["European Union Foreign Ministers","https://www.euronews.com/2026/10/12/eu-agrees-new-security-mission-in-lebanon-as-un-peacekeepers-prepare-to-leave"]," approved a new civilian-military mission aimed at advising and training the Lebanese military and police personnel."]},{"k":"p","s":["But these appear to be limited operations that aren’t designed to replace UNIFIL."]},{"k":"p","s":["U.S. President Donald Trump, meanwhile, has previously ",["proposed that Syria enter Lebanon","https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/us-should-not-encourage-syria-enter-lebanon"]," to help end the violence and execute “a surgical attack on Hezbollah.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["But given the past experience of Syrian occupation, this proposal would certainly not be welcomed by the Lebanese people."]},{"k":"h","s":["Remaining questions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although Lebanese security is a paramount concern regarding the impending withdrawal of UNIFIL, it’s not the only potential risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["UNIFIL ",["employs many Lebanese civilians and is a steady consumer of local goods and services","https://english.aawsat.com/arab-world/5331838-end-unifil-mission-impacts-hundreds-jobs-south-lebanon"],". It also provides humanitarian aid to the region."]},{"k":"p","s":["And the departure of UNIFIL may further erode Lebanon sovereignty by opening the door for the sharing of security by neighbors – and former occupiers – Syria and Israel."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is all the more worrying given that the ",["Lebanese armed forces","https://www.thenationalnews.com/opinion/comment/2026/03/24/theres-only-so-much-lebanons-army-can-do-against-hezbollah-it-needs-to-make-every-move-count/"]," are poorly trained, have a poor record as a fighting force and will be susceptible to attacks from Hezbollah fighters and sympathizers, which might trigger sectarian violence in the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the vacuum left by U.N. forces, some Lebanese military personnel might simply decide that opposing Hezbollah is too risky altogether."]},{"k":"p","s":["All of these factors make what comes next for Lebanon after UNIFIL’s departure as opaque as it is worrying for those concerned about the country’s well-being."]},{"k":"p","s":["For the sake of Lebanese sovereignty, the twin goals of disarming Hezbollah and ensuring Israel’s withdrawal from Lebanese territory remain paramount. Yet it’s unclear who will take on those duties absent a stronger Lebanese state."]},{"k":"p","s":["For all of its inability to prevent wars, invasions and violations of ceasefires, UNIFIL’s presence guaranteed a degree of Lebanese sovereignty over its lands and served as a reminder of the international community’s commitment to peace in the region. An effective replacement force would at least have to do the same."]}],"chars":7790},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/liberia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"7f033f1c3af37cca27649c826e64d1cc3f954cb9415eb9c82b267bfffb01c4b5","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/liberia","bytes":56381,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Liberia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-28T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-11T16:56:17+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/liberia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Liberia’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Liberia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Liberian Embassy in the UK","https://www.embassyofliberia.co.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Liberia, your passport must be valid for the duration of your stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to enter Liberia."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Apply for a visa to visit Liberia through the ",["Liberian Embassy in the UK","https://www.embassyofliberia.co.uk/consular-services/visa-application/"],". Your passport must have at least 6 months validity remaining when you make your application."]},{"k":"p","s":["Once in Liberia, you can apply for an extension of your existing visa for a maximum of 60 days. To apply for an extension in Monrovia, you should visit the Immigration Office at Gbarngaye’s Town, 24th Street, Sinkor. You’ll need to take your passport and pay a fee of 25 US dollars."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re staying outside Montserrado County, contact your local Immigration Office to get a visa extension authorised by the Immigration County Commander."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer you must ",["apply for a Liberian residence permit","https://lis.gov.lr/service"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["As part of the visa application process, you need to provide a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, including yellow fever, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Liberia guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/128/liberia#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola Screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 25 May, the Liberia Ministry of Health and National Public Health Institute issued a ",["Travel Advisory note","https://onehealthliberia.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Public-Health-Travel-Advisory-NO-01-1-1.pdf"]," announcing heighted health screening measures for travellers arriving into Liberia, due to outbreaks of Ebola in Uganda and the Democratic Republic of Congo. Travellers may be asked to submit health information on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["The World Health Organisation (WHO) have ",["declared this a Public Health Emergency of International Concern","https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Liberia. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 US dollars or more."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money out of Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take more than 7,500 US dollars in cash out of the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there is no recent history of terrorism in Liberia, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist groups in West Africa continue to mount attacks in the region. Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places frequented by foreign nationals, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["beach resorts"]},{"k":"li","s":["hotels"]},{"k":"li","s":["cafés and restaurants"]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Liberia is generally stable politically, but protests and demonstrations do take place and, particularly small-scale protests, can begin with little or no warning. Avoid protests, demonstrations, political rallies and large crowds. Follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["The crime rate in Liberia is high. Liberians are the main victims of crime, but foreign nationals can also be targeted. Most crime is opportunistic theft, but there are organised criminal gangs. Thieves are often armed with knives or machetes, and occasionally carry firearms."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals have been mugged in the Mamba Point and Sinkor areas of Monrovia, where most international visitors stay. Criminals also operate in nightclubs and on beaches during the day."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreigners have reported assault and robbery happening in taxis."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take steps to reduce your personal risk by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["not carrying valuables or large sums of money in public"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding walking around Monrovia after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding isolated areas after dark, including beaches"]},{"k":"li","s":["only staying in reputable accommodation with suitable security arrangements"]},{"k":"li","s":["arranging for transport, including to and from the airport, in advance"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Liberia has a high rate of rape and sexual assault. There have been cases of rapes and attempted rapes of foreign women, although these are rare."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Carry photo ID with you at all times, such as your driving licence or a copy of the photo page of your passport. You may be asked to show it at any time by immigration officials or the police."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drug trafficking and diamond smuggling"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drug trafficking and gold and diamond smuggling are illegal. If convicted, you will get a long prison sentence. Local prison conditions are harsh."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is illegal in Liberia."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Adoption in Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ministry of Justice in Liberia can issue a decree of adoption if all legal requirements are met. However, adoption orders from Liberia are not recognised in the UK. If you’re returning to live in the UK, you’ll need to apply for entry clearance for the child as a Liberian child coming for adoption in the UK."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Liberia remains a predominantly cash-based society, especially with street vendors."]},{"k":"p","s":["Credit and debit cards are not generally accepted in Liberia, except for a few of the major hotels and some larger supermarkets. They may be unable to process card payments when internet connections are poor. Not many places accept travellers cheques. Mobile money apps offer an alternative way to make payments, but not all vendors use these."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are a few ATMs that accept Visa cards in Monrovia, including in the two main hotels used by international visitors. ATMs often run out of money or only have certain denominations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign exchange and banking facilities are limited. Western Union and MoneyGram can transfer money from the UK, although there are very few offices outside Monrovia."]},{"k":"p","s":["Liberia’s banks can experience cash shortages of both US dollars and Liberian dollars. Banks and foreign exchange facilities may not be able to pay out transferred funds or fulfil ATM withdrawals. Bring sufficient funds, in US dollars, to cover all expenses."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming and water safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Beaches in Liberia can have rip tides and other dangerous currents. Beaches and the sea in Monrovia city centre are dirty. Get local advice before going in the water."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid canoes and fishing boats offering passenger services. They are regularly overwhelmed by strong waves and currents."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sporting events"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Samuel K Doe Stadium in Monrovia can become overcrowded during major football matches or events."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re planning to drive in Liberia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get security advice about travelling by road anywhere outside Monrovia."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most foreign nationals travelling by road outside Monrovia will hire a car with a driver, and travel in convoy of at least 2 vehicles in case of breakdowns or possible security incidents."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions and driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["The roads from Monrovia to the port town of Buchanan and to the border with Sierra Leone at Bo Waterside, and to the border with Guinea at Ganta, are mainly paved and in reasonable condition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most other roads outside Monrovia are unpaved. Roads outside Monrovia can be treacherous and most are unlit."]},{"k":"p","s":["The road from Monrovia to Roberts International Airport is currently undergoing roadworks. It can be hazardous and prone to severe traffic."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving and road conditions deteriorate significantly during the rainy season from May to November, and many roads become impassable."]},{"k":"p","s":["The standard of driving is generally poor, and many vehicles do not have lights. Drivers often swerve to avoid potholes and taxis can slow or stop unpredictably to pick up or drop off passengers. Motorcycle taxis (‘pein-peins’) are particularly dangerous."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in a traffic accident, be aware that local crowds can become hostile and may attempt to attack you."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Liberian National Police and other security authorities operate checkpoints on roads throughout the country. You’re more vulnerable to being robbed at an illegal checkpoint at night."]},{"k":"p","s":["Pull over to the side of the road immediately when instructed by security forces travelling with VIP convoys."]},{"k":"h","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["UK Air Safety List (ASL)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list#liberia"]," lists all known airlines in Liberia that do not meet international safety standards and are banned from operating commercial air services to, from, and within the UK. Check the UK Air Safety List when considering which airlines to fly with. The list is maintained by the Department for Transport, based on advice from the ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.caa.co.uk/Commercial-Industry/Airlines/Licensing/Requirements-and-guidance/Third-Country-Operator-Certificates"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Liberia. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/liberia"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/liberia/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Liberia-Côte d’Ivoire border"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are occasional clashes between armed groups from both sides of the Liberia-Côte d’Ivoire border in some of the more remote border areas of Grand Gedeh and River Gee counties. Use the main roads when travelling in these areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Sinoe rubber plantation and Sapo National Park"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Liberian authorities have limited ability to provide emergency help outside Monrovia. Check the security situation before travelling to any part of the country. Land disputes, illegal mining and occupation of rubber plantations can lead to violent incidents, particularly in rural areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["There may be organised groups of former combatants in areas of the country where there is limited government security presence. Be especially wary in the Sinoe rubber plantation and Sapo National Park."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no reliable ambulance service in Liberia. If you need emergency medical treatment, you should make your own way to hospital. You can also get advice from your hotel or the local police."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Liberia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/128/liberia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see how to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Liberia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/128/liberia"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["typhoid"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also a risk of Lassa fever in Liberia, with several confirmed cases in 2026. Check ",["TravelHealthPro’s information on Lassa fever","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/68/viral-haemorrhagic-fever"],". If you’re concerned that you might have been exposed to, or are showing symptoms of Lassa fever, get immediate medical advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["The 2025 UNAIDS Spectrum data estimates that approximately 36,000 people are living with HIV in Liberia. This translates to a prevalence percentage of around 0.9% of the adult population. You should use normal precautions to avoid exposure to HIV/AIDS."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medication is usually sold over the counter in pharmacies, without a prescription. Supplies can be unreliable and medications available in the UK may not be available in Liberia. Make sure you have adequate supplies of prescription medication, bearing in mind the risk of delays due to flight cancellations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Hospitals and medical facilities throughout Liberia are poorly equipped. Blood supplies are unreliable and unsafe, and quality medication is scarce."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no effective public or private accident and emergency or ambulance service anywhere in the country. Carry basic medical supplies with you."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad, medical evacuation and repatriation. Most health care providers only accept cash payments."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Liberia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/liberia-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: +231 770 800 911"]},{"k":"p","s":["Emergency services are unreliable in Liberia."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=liberia"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/liberia-funeral-directors"]," in Liberia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Liberia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/liberia-bereavement-death-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Liberia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/liberia-prisoners-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/liberia/british-embassy-monrovia"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/liberia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":15677,"change":"Updated information about heightened health screening measures for travellers arriving into Liberia (See 'Ebola screening' on the ‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/which-is-a-more-effective-deterrent-to-speeding-drivers-fines-or-demerit-points-our-study-found-out-296814":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-26T00:27Z","sha":"cfe4915e73df6e60acf93ac7c06931d613c8a55107fabab19690a9ba41acbc77","read":"https://theconversation.com/which-is-a-more-effective-deterrent-to-speeding-drivers-fines-or-demerit-points-our-study-found-out-296814","bytes":179476,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Which is a more effective deterrent to speeding drivers – fines or demerit points? Our study found out","by":[{"n":"Romain Gauriot","r":"Associate professor, Deakin University"},{"n":"Maria Wisniewska","r":"Associate Research Fellow, Deakin University"}],"pub":"2026-11-25T23:53:20Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Every year, almost 1500 people ",["die on Australian roads","https://www.aihw.gov.au/reports/injury/transport-accidents"],", and around 65,000 are hospitalised. The annual social and economic costs of these crashes is ",["an estimated","https://datahub.roadsafety.gov.au/reporting/social-cost-road-crashes"]," $22-30 billion."]},{"k":"p","s":["To improve road safety, governments try to motivate safe driving through a mix of fines and/or ",["demerit points","https://www.qld.gov.au/transport/safety/fines/demerit/about"]," (penalties drivers accumulate that may lead to license suspension)."]},{"k":"p","s":["These penalties aim to make roads safer by punishing those who speed and deterring drivers from offending in the first place."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our ",["recent research","https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.09337"]," – using Queensland Department of Transport and Main Roads data – investigated whether fines or demerit point reductions impacted driver behaviour the most. During this study, we also learnt about a growing number of drivers selling their demerit points online to avoid license suspensions."]},{"k":"h","s":["Fines vs. demerit points"]},{"k":"p","s":["Each state and territory government sets the levels of their penalties independently. Current fines for minor offences range from ",["$100 in Western Australia","https://www.transport.wa.gov.au/licensing/drivers-licence/driver-rules-penalties-infringements/driving-offences"]," to ",["$345 in Queensland","https://www.qld.gov.au/transport/safety/fines/demerit/points"]," and ",["$394","https://www.legislation.act.gov.au/sl/2005-11/"]," in the Australian Capital Territory."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Queensland, there were around ",["800,000 speeding infringements","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-10-30/qld-drivers-fined-almost-1-million-dollars-every-day-speeding/103036604"]," in 2022-23, amounting to $332 million. Around ",["75%","https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.09337"]," of the penalties come from “occasional” speedsters – those speeding once in three years. The other 25% comes from repeat offenders – drivers who might be at risk of license suspension through demerit point penalties."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most speeding offences – ",["84%","https://datahub.roadsafety.gov.au/driver-behaviour/police-enforcement#anchor-explore-the-data"]," nationally and ",["86%","https://datahub.roadsafety.gov.au/driver-behaviour/police-enforcement#anchor-explore-the-data"]," in Queensland – are detected by cameras, which record the vehicle rather than the person behind the wheel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Unlike a roadside police stop, where an officer can confirm who is driving, a camera notice is sent to the registered vehicle owner."]},{"k":"p","s":["To make sure the penalty reaches the right person, there is a process that lets an owner who was not driving at the time transfer the fine, along with its demerit points, to the actual driver."]},{"k":"p","s":["Each year, ",["around 1.6%","https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.09337"]," of all camera-issued fines in Queensland are transferred this way."]},{"k":"h","s":["What our research found"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Our research","https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.09337"]," examined how fines and penalty points affected drivers’ behaviours."]},{"k":"p","s":["We evaluated the effects of a sharp penalty increase in Queensland in 2022, which was introduced as part of ",["a statewide strategy","https://statements.qld.gov.au/statements/94934"]," to reduce the number of road deaths in the state to zero by 2050."]},{"k":"p","s":["The strategy increased fines for all drivers. It also narrowed its lowest speeding tier (one demerit point) from 1-12km/h over the limit to 1-10km/h. Drivers caught speeding 11 or 12 km/h over the limit moved into the 11-20km/h bracket (rising from one to three demerit points). For these drivers, the risk of license suspension became substantially higher overnight, jumping from one to three demerit points."]},{"k":"p","s":["We found drivers responded to the increased penalties in two ways:"]},{"k":"li","s":["receiving a higher penalty led to a decrease in subsequent offences. Interestingly, the effect is driven entirely by the rise in demerit points; higher fines did not affect drivers’ behaviour. This suggests drivers are more concerned about possibly losing their licence than paying a larger fine"]},{"k":"li","s":["drivers responded to the increase in demerit points by transferring more fines – most commonly when there was lower-quality photographic evidence, suggesting potential abuse of the system."]},{"k":"p","s":["With the supposedly low-risk nomination process, drivers can be incentivised to transfer the liability to someone else."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our research is backed up by a ",["recent ABC report","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-08/inside-thriving-black-market-trading-demerit-points-for-cash/107320430"]," that uncovered a scheme where drivers sell their demerit points online to avoid license suspension. In one extreme case, a Queensland woman allegedly accumulated more than 1,000 demerit points through transfers, far beyond the 12-point limit that normally triggers a licence suspension, before being charged with fraud."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ideas for making our roads safer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Queensland’s reform shows demerit points can effectively reduce speeding. Other jurisdictions could benefit from a similar approach."]},{"k":"p","s":["As policymakers consider strengthening these penalties, it will be important to watch for side effects, such as drivers falsely transferring their points to someone else, or extra pressure on people who depend on their car for work."]},{"k":"p","s":["Making sure each penalty reaches the person who was actually driving is vital."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beyond adjusting demerit points, governments have other tools to make speeding penalties both effective and fair."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another idea is income-based fines, tied to drivers’ salaries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fines can play an important role in discouraging speeding, but the same fine is much more damaging to a lower income driver."]},{"k":"p","s":["Income-based fines, which link the penalty to what a driver earns, offer one way to address this: ",["evidence suggests","https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag071"]," they can reduce speeding while spreading the burden more evenly."]},{"k":"h","s":["Where to from here?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Australia’s road toll is a major public health and safety issue and one which governments across the nation wrestle with constantly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Any initiatives to combat dangerous driving are therefore welcomed, although our research suggests demerit points might move the needle more than other forms of punishments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Authorities will just need to be wary if there’s any unexpected consequences, including people selling their demerit points."]}],"chars":5075},"https://theconversation.com/we-built-an-ai-you-can-dance-with-it-taught-us-about-developing-the-technology-responsibly-295348":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-26T21:19Z","sha":"1613747779e591dadb2ac5c3ad1cbf2c7ae7c23a68bf7a84c97f7231c809cd3b","read":"https://theconversation.com/we-built-an-ai-you-can-dance-with-it-taught-us-about-developing-the-technology-responsibly-295348","bytes":186911,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"We built an AI you can dance with. It taught us about developing the technology responsibly","by":[{"n":"Charlotta Windahl","r":"Senior Lecturer in Management and International Business, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"},{"n":"Sarah Foster-Sproull","r":"Associate Professor, Creative Arts, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"},{"n":"Yinan Liu","r":"Research Associate, arc/sec Lab, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"}],"pub":"2026-11-26T19:15:14Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["In recent months, top researchers ",["have quit","https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/security/two-ai-researchers-leave-anthropic-google-safety-concerns-rcna597086"]," leading artificial intelligence companies, warning the AI race is moving too quickly, with too little oversight and accountability."]},{"k":"p","s":["When those building this technology ",["sound the alarm","https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/2026/10/openai-safety-team-resignation/688881/"],", we should pay attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["But more than warnings, what’s needed are working examples of how AI could be developed differently. In an Auckland dance studio, we’ve found one."]},{"k":"p","s":["Over ten months, our team built an AI dance partner in “extended reality” (which blends the physical world with digital elements) as part of ",["our laboratory’s research","https://www.arc-sec.com/info"]," into the relationships between people and ",["digital-physical environments","https://gam.tugraz.at/issues/gam-22/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["We wanted to know whether an architectural avatar – a digital structure that moved in response to a dancer – could become an improvisational dance partner, and what would make it a good one."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dancing with an AI"]},{"k":"p","s":["A choreographer, an architectural designer and two professional dancers developed and tested our AI dance partner."]},{"k":"p","s":["The dancers wore motion-capture suits tracked by ten cameras. Where ",["large language models","https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/large-language-models"]," such as ChatGPT ",["learn from text","https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7842364-how-chatgpt-and-our-foundation-models-are-developed"],", our generative AI learned from their movements. The dancers met their partner through a headset blending digital images with the real room, while the rest of us watched on screens."]},{"k":"p","s":["Improvisational dance is a tough test for AI. Partners read each other in fractions of a second, through movement, breath, rhythm and weight. This let us test whether a machine could respond like part of a duet. We worked out what mattered, and to whom, by moving with the AI as we developed it."]},{"k":"p","s":["One dancer wasn’t enough. The AI had to be trained on a duet because what we wanted it to learn lived in the space between the dancers. We were composing a relationship."]},{"k":"p","s":["A small set of choreographed duets taught the system better than a large library of generic movement. Tango gave it a structure it could read, but limited the dancers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Improvisation confused it, and it struggled with floor work, central to much contemporary dance in Aotearoa. Sticks extending from the dancers’ elbows made the patterns of distance and direction between them more readable."]},{"k":"p","s":["For most of the ten months, the partner only followed. Training it to offer anything back took patient work. Mixing dance styles produced bland, in-between moves."]},{"k":"p","s":["When the system stopped responding to a dancer’s small gestures, the dancer stopped making them."]},{"k":"p","s":["This concerned us. If AI routinely ignores what we bring to it – our movements, writing, judgements or minority views – we may gradually stop contributing them. Over time, what we think and create could become limited to what the machine recognises and rewards."]},{"k":"p","s":["The studio work left us with questions the technology cannot answer. Whose movement should the system learn from, which kinds, and how much? What makes a good partner, and a relationship worth having? And who gets to be the choreographer?"]},{"k":"h","s":["Being ‘response-able’"]},{"k":"p","s":["Models such as ChatGPT are built to talk and write like a person. We built our dance partner as a responsive space, something people move with. The dancers whose movement trained the system also tested and judged it, shaping what it became."]},{"k":"p","s":["Recordings stayed on local encrypted drives, the dancers retained ownership of their data, and any choreography was credited as a joint creation."]},{"k":"p","s":["When the people who know the work stay close to a system, they become what feminist philosopher and science studies scholar ",["Donna Haraway","https://www.dukeupress.edu/Staying-with-the-Trouble"]," calls “ ",["response-able","https://www.youtube.com/shorts/au6eRbODqP0"],"”: responsive to the system and one another, and accountable for what happens."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bring a skilled person into the loop and the system rises to meet them. Build it to serve everyone at once, and the responses drift toward a bland, generic in-between."]},{"k":"p","s":["The companies racing toward “ ",["superintelligence","https://www.forbes.com/sites/technology/article/ai-superintelligence/"],"” work from the opposite logic. Add more data, computing power and scale, and intelligence will appear. Their models learn from vast quantities of scraped material, relevant and irrelevant."]},{"k":"p","s":["The writers, news agencies and artists whose work trains AI often have little say in what the models become. Built from that scraped mix and tuned to be agreeable, the models can favour widely represented views over less common perspectives, even when those perspectives are correct."]},{"k":"p","s":["When people with the knowledge to notice and judge are absent from how these models are built and used, ",["accountability can spread so thinly","https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2026/09/28/is-ai-above-the-law"]," that no one holds it."]},{"k":"h","s":["What could NZ do differently?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The size of the model may not be what matters most. A small country can build and reward AI specific to its users."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Māori data sovereignty","https://www.temanararaunga.maori.nz/patai"],", advanced by groups such as ",["Te Mana Raraunga","https://www.temanararaunga.maori.nz/tutohinga"],", a network advocating Māori rights and interests in data, wants data to stay connected and accountable to the communities it comes from. Our dance study points the same way."]},{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand has favoured a relatively light-touch approach to AI regulation, relying largely on existing laws and voluntary guidance."]},{"k":"p","s":["But a government choosing a different path could give skilled people a real say in how AI is trained and used in health, justice, education and welfare."]},{"k":"p","s":["It could ",["hold AI companies responsible","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/01/technology/ai-rogue-agents-liability.html"]," for their systems after release, and require them to account for costs they may otherwise pass on to the public, including ",["the use of data","https://www.techpolicy.press/data-scraping-makes-ai-systems-possible-but-at-whose-expense/"]," and the ",["energy","https://www.iea.org/reports/key-questions-on-energy-and-ai"]," and ",["water","https://collections.unu.edu/eserv/UNU:10647/UNU-INWEH-Report-The_Env_Cost_of_AI-2026.pdf"]," their systems consume. It could also fund shared tools so communities can build AI systems that answer to them."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our study offers one small example of an alternative approach to AI development. The challenge now is to ensure our institutions recognise and reward such alternatives."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authors acknowledge Uwe Rieger, founder and co-director of the University of Auckland-based arc/sec Lab; Katy Mason, University of Salford; and two professional dancers."]}],"chars":5686}};
